WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.260
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today.

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:07.560
Tim Maudlin, who's the founder and director
of the John Bell Institute for the Foundations

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.880
of Physics and also professor of philosophy
at New York University.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.690
Really appreciate your time today.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.120
Speaker 8: Thanks for having me.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.420
Speaker 1: So let's let's get into metaphysics
a little bit and give some some in our audience

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.110
some tools with which to understand this concept.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:27.949
Very often, people on the Internet love making
videos about metaphysics, and they seem to

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:30.410
have no idea what we're even talking about.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:34.520
So so, you know, one analogy that I've used
and you tell me whether this gets us in the

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:43.090
right direction, if we want to understand
how the moon got to exist and where it is,

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:50.050
gravity would be part of of understanding
that metaphysics would look at how did gravity

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.250
get created?

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.500
Was there a before gravity?

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:59.250
Is that is that an example of what we talk
about when we say physics versus metaphysics?

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:04.650
Speaker 8: Well, unfortunately, just the language
physics and metaphysics makes it sound like

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.671
what you just said.

00:01:05.671 --> 00:01:08.110
And a lot of people use it that way.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.400
Historically, that's not what it is at all.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.400
Okay.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.040
Metaphysics, there was a document that came
down to us from Aristotle that a later editor

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.100
called The Metaphysics.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.150
Aristotle never used the term.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:24.689
And what he's doing in that is looking at
what we call ontology or just the genesis

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:30.190
general theory of what exists carried out
at the most general high level scale.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.190
Right.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.780
So not gee, is there a you know, is there
a rock over here?

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.829
But are there rocks at all?

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:37.299
And what are rocks made of?

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:43.240
So from that point of view, it's not that
you go beyond physics to do metaphysics.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:48.760
I actually have a book called The Metaphysics
Within Physics, because physics makes postulates

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.760
about, at a fundamental level, what exists.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.270
That's part of metaphysics.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.070
Now, maybe not everything is physical, so
metaphysics would go further than that if

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.289
there are nonphysical things.

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:04.210
But part of it is just the basic picture of
what exists physically.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.229
Now, gravity exists physically.

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:07.229
Okay.

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:09.280
Yeah, gravity is part of it.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.100
Is there a story about where gravity came
from?

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.100
Maybe.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.100
Maybe not.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.830
Maybe that's just kind of ground level stuff
once you get down to the law of gravity.

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:19.580
There's no further account of that.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.500
That's the bottom level of everything.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:28.400
Speaker 1: When we think about things like
the relationship between mind and body, which

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:36.180
can have religious aspects to the discussion,
scientific, philosophical, consciousness,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.000
free will, these sorts of things.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.500
Are these all conversations that include both
what we might consider traditional hard science

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.250
as well as metaphysics?

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:51.920
Or are they only that depending on the preconceived
notions one brings to the conversation?

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.220
Speaker 8: No, I mean the sorts of questions
you're asking.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:02.550
Certainly the mind body problem involves fundamental
metaphysical questions if you think there

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:05.360
is a nonphysical soul.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:06.450
Some people do.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.450
Some people don't.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:11.690
If you do, then there's an issue of how it
can interact with a physical body, right?

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.410
And that the interaction would have to go
both ways.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:20.110
If you think there is and then you have to
say, well, how is it that just physical stuff

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:23.450
doing something could give rise to consciousness
and awareness.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.180
That's a very deep problem as well.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.680
So there's no easy answers here.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:33.580
There are different answers, and each answer
kind of involves its own questions that that

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.610
that run on from it.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:40.909
So, yeah, I mean, metaphysics certainly gets
involved in those kinds of questions.

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:47.459
Speaker 1: When I've talked to scientists
about the question what was there before the

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:53.360
universe, the answers obviously are always
couched in we don't actually know.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:59.709
But one question is, was there really a before
the universe, which is a hard thing to think

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:03.760
about because it seems, if you understand
time linearly, that there must have been some

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.019
before or some beginning or something like
that.

00:04:07.019 --> 00:04:10.800
What what does metaphysics have to add to
that question?

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.780
Speaker 8: Well, it's not that metaphysics
is going to ride to the rescue and provide

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:17.780
answers to this.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:21.510
I mean, the questions you ask again are good
questions.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:27.790
Is there anything before, let's say, this
universe now, one possible answer to that

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.110
is, yeah, there was another universe and before
that, maybe another one and before that maybe

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:32.110
another.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:34.729
And then there's a question of how one arises
out of another.

00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:35.900
Maybe you say as you say.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:41.440
One possible answer is, Look, there was no
before this universe, and that might be because

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:42.729
time goes on forever.

00:04:42.729 --> 00:04:45.680
Or it might be the time doesn't even go on
forever.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.070
But nonetheless, there's only so much of it.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:50.750
You sort of there's only a limited amount.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:53.270
This is the kind of standard big Bang theory.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.910
You go back 13.7 billion years and there is
nothing before that.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.311
So it's not that that came out.

00:04:59.311 --> 00:05:00.870
Of anything earlier.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.550
There was no earlier.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.910
These are different answers.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:04.910
Right.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:11.030
And cosmologists have proposed different theories
that answer these questions in different ways.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:15.440
It's not as if metaphysician have some magic
wand that they're going to give you the right

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.440
answer.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.940
If you're well-trained in philosophy, you
can at least delineate, look, there's this

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:20.940
answer.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:21.940
There's this answer, There's this answer.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:23.160
Let's see how they differ.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.830
Let's see what observations might bear on
this and questions like that.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:32.400
Speaker 1: I think one of the areas where
laypeople sometimes come up against I don't

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.720
know if frustration or despair or existential
crises is the right term, but with some of

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:44.289
these questions, what becomes tough is that
it can seem as though you either have to say,

00:05:44.289 --> 00:05:49.250
I'm going to try to answer some of these questions
simply with science, because that's the only

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:53.420
place where the scientific method exists,
and that's all I'm comfortable with in terms

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.979
of confidence in what we either know or don't
know.

00:05:57.979 --> 00:06:05.680
Or I have to look at a different path altogether,
which would be philosophy, metaphysics, etc..

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.820
And that can be frustrating because some people
feel like I'm kind of pre selecting where

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:15.500
the end point for me is going to be in some
of these questions by choosing one of these

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.900
two paths, almost like a fork in the road.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.169
Is that an understandable frustration?

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:24.310
But more importantly, is it an accurate critique
or observation?

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:25.310
Maybe is the right word?

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:29.210
Speaker 8: So I think you have something particular
in mind there.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:33.919
And let me just point it out, because you
say, oh, there's the scientific way and then

00:06:33.919 --> 00:06:35.130
there are other ways.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:39.419
Speaker 1: Well, I'm saying if someone feels
that way, are they right or is that not the

00:06:39.419 --> 00:06:41.509
right interpretation or understanding?

00:06:41.509 --> 00:06:46.630
Speaker 8: The first thing I would say is
sort of any discipline where you provide what

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:52.699
we think is good reasons to believe what you're
proposing counts is a kind of science sometimes

00:06:52.699 --> 00:06:55.190
that's experimental or observational.

00:06:55.190 --> 00:07:00.110
But notice when we divide the university into
the arts and sciences, the math departments

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.530
in the sciences, right?

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:06.919
Yeah, it works on observations, but because
it works by arguments, by proofs.

00:07:06.919 --> 00:07:07.919
Right?

00:07:07.919 --> 00:07:13.570
You say, Look, I have a reason that I can
give to you for for accepting this answer.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:19.259
From that point of view, anything that really
has good evidence, any anybody that's putting

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:24.789
forward good evidence for what they're claiming
is doing science, whatever else you call it.

00:07:24.789 --> 00:07:28.650
And if something doesn't count as science,
then you're just saying, well, I'm putting

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.520
forward opinions and I have no good reasons
for them.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.090
And I would say, well, that's not such a great
thing to do.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:35.960
Now, there are limits to science.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:41.389
There may be things that you'd like to know
that we're just not in a position to know.

00:07:41.389 --> 00:07:42.510
And if there are, there are.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:46.340
I mean, you just have to live, you know, live
with the limitations of the world.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:52.460
Speaker 1: What are, in your mind, some of
the most interesting questions or problems

00:07:52.460 --> 00:08:01.500
that are being looked at in in terms of metaphysics
and philosophy right now that maybe may be

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:03.780
