00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:39.030
They're very interesting.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:42.610
I mean, I could go into detailed questions
there, but I'm sure that would take us beyond

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:45.170
the scope of this this interview.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:52.171
But I work in foundations of physics, so of
course, I tend to those there are people who

00:08:52.171 --> 00:08:54.290
are worried about how did life begin?

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.290
Right.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:59.950
That's how how could living things have would
have have come out of non-living things.

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:01.530
And that's a very interesting question.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:07.300
I mean, and I've heard interesting answers
having to do with how clay forms and, you

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:13.529
know, various kind of inorganic structures
that could help create the sorts of complexity

00:09:13.529 --> 00:09:15.890
that we need for life and so on.

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:17.120
That would be in biology.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:19.170
An interesting question.

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:21.440
So there are lots of them in different places.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.290
I happen to live in the in the physics part
of it.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:31.579
Speaker 1: I recently read a couple of books
about solipsism, and one of the claims that

