00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:23.120
etc..

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:26.899
What if someone who do the does the work you
do think about such an assertion?

00:10:26.899 --> 00:10:27.899
Speaker 7: Well.

00:10:27.899 --> 00:10:31.670
Speaker 8: Let me just check because there's
a little linguistic question I have.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.790
You said you read books about solipsism.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:35.970
Did you mean that or skepticism?

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:37.290
Because it sounded like you were right.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:38.760
Speaker 1: I meant solipsism.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.459
Speaker 8: You meant solipsism.

00:10:40.459 --> 00:10:42.810
Well, solipsism is a view.

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:44.060
It's a very definite view.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:48.060
It sort of says the only thing that exists
is me.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:49.180
I mean, that.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:50.180
That.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:51.290
Okay, that's a possible view.

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:52.290
It's kind of crazy.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:54.500
I mean, there's so.

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:58.550
Speaker 1: Even someone like you is comfortable
refuting it by saying it's kind of crazy.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:04.811
Speaker 8: Well, you would say if you want
by a refutation and absolute proof, you're

00:11:04.811 --> 00:11:06.060
not going to get it.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.060
Okay.

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:11.010
It's not that the solipsism has an absolute
proof of any sort that solipsism is true.

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:15.150
So you can't you can't give that standard
